          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582783,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Early Years and Military Career</strong><br>\nAnwar Sadat was born on December 25, 1918, in Mit Abul Kom, Egypt. Coming from a modest background, Sadat graduated from the Royal Military Academy and served in the Egyptian army. He was a part of the Free Officers Movement, which overthrew King Farouk in 1952.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Rise in Egyptian Politics</strong><br>\nAfter the revolution, Sadat held several high-ranking positions, including editor of the newly established revolutionary newspaper Al-Gomhuria. His political career progressed under President Gamal Abdel Nasser, where he served in various capacities, including Vice President.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Presidency and Economic Policies</strong><br>\nSadat became President of Egypt following Nasser's death in 1970. He implemented \"Infitah,\" an open-door policy to revitalize Egypt's economy through foreign investment and to end the Soviet influence in Egypt.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "53bc5361-5ce5-43a9-a00f-57bbceee9cd0"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          }
        ],
        "slideBackgroundSelector": {}
      },
      {
        "elements": [
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
