            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          },
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582782,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>The October War</strong><br>\nIn 1973, Sadat launched a surprise attack against Israel on Yom Kippur to reclaim territory lost during the 1967 Six-Day War. The conflict led to increased U.S. involvement in the region and eventually to a peace process.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Path to Peace with Israel</strong><br>\nSadat made a historic visit to Jerusalem in 1977, addressing the Israeli Knesset and calling for peace. This led to intense negotiations, resulting in the Camp David Accords and the subsequent Egyptian-Israeli Peace Treaty in 1979.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Nobel Peace Prize and Domestic Reforms</strong><br>\nFor his efforts to achieve peace, Sadat was awarded the Nobel Peace Prize, shared with Israeli Prime Minister Menachem Begin in 1978. Domestically, Sadat faced opposition due to his economic policies and peace treaty with Israel.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3b13867b-feab-4e2e-bfa2-fcc82e4dbff1"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
